 "narrative": "All twelve delivered estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction and are compared with the 0.15 composite-dependency-unit absolute threshold. H124, H125, and H126 test SMARCA4-loss stronger dependency in MSI-nonhigh samples with high EMT, stemness, or 8q24 copy gain: signed estimates +0.821 (95% CI +0.683 to +0.958), +0.618 (+0.474 to +0.763), and +0.836 (+0.719 to +0.952), respectively. Each full interval exceeds threshold in the stronger-dependency direction, so I accept and close them. H127 and H128 test TP53-loss and KEAP1-loss weaker dependency in high-hypoxia and high IFN-gamma contexts, respectively: signed estimates +0.505 (+0.319 to +0.690) and +0.756 (+0.619 to +0.893). Both intervals clear threshold in the weaker-dependency direction, supporting robustness of these accepted central associations in those biological-state contexts. H129 through H133 test MSI-high stronger dependency in top-quartile EMT, stemness, 8q24 copy gain, hypoxia, and IFN-gamma samples: signed estimates +0.654 (+0.424 to +0.885), +0.780 (+0.548 to +1.012), +0.830 (+0.613 to +1.047), +0.840 (+0.572 to +1.109), and +0.817 (+0.649 to +0.985). Although exposed MSI-high cells are modest (28-57), all cells meet the minimum-20 rule and all intervals remain well above the practical threshold. I accept and close them, while noting they remain dataset-internal conditional associations rather than independent replication. H134 and H135 test TP53-loss and KEAP1-loss weaker dependency in high-ploidy MSI-nonhigh samples: signed estimates +0.378 (+0.176 to +0.579) and +0.913 (+0.787 to +1.038). Both clear the threshold; H134 is the least conservative because its lower bound is closer to threshold, but the point estimate is still substantially above 0.15. I accept and close both. Critical-review considerations: these twelve comparisons are simultaneous robustness probes, so multiplicity is relevant. However, the magnitude of the effects and the fact that all lower bounds exceed the effect-size threshold reduce concern that the acceptances are primarily false-positive multiplicity artifacts. The analyses use measured top-quartile biological-state variables and assumed they are non-outcome-derived and non-collinear; without further model-based adjustment or independent replication, they should be interpreted as descriptive subgroup associations, not causal effects, not single-gene-knockout mechanism specificity, and not external generalizability claims. No validation request is made because the remaining voluntary validation budget is zero and the global central predictor effects have already been independently validated; these results refine boundary conditions for already validated central associations.",
 "research_notes": "Iteration 11 appraise state: all 12 active context-robustness comparisons H124-H135 are accepted and closed. The validated central map is now internally robust across top-quartile EMT, stemness, 8q24 copy gain, hypoxia, IFN-gamma, and high ploidy contexts. Specifically, SMARCA4 loss and MSI-high remain associated with stronger composite dependency, while TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss remain associated with weaker composite dependency, in these biological-state and chromosomal-instability contexts. H134 (TP53 loss in high ploidy MSI-nonhigh) is the least conservative because its lower bound is close to the 0.15 threshold, but it still clears both point-estimate and interval criteria. No further validation can be requested. The mature interpretation is descriptive and dataset-internal: broad central association plus context robustness, with no evidence that constructed research markers or most conventional technical/contextual variables alter the central map. Further near-threshold PTEN subgroup mining should remain avoided. If future analyses are added, they should be limited to genuinely boundary-conditioning or confounding-identification questions rather than additional near-threshold subgroup selection."
